[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified the inter-company link and routing end-to-end. The BGP peering across the partner link (router1:eth0 193.10.11.1 <-> router2:eth0 193.10.11.2) is stable: both sides show “BGP state = Established” with Up/Down ~2 minutes, “Connections established 1; dropped 0,” and continuous KEEPALIVEs. FRR logs show normal updates and no recurring resets beyond initial startup. At Layer 2, ethtool reports link detected on both eth0s, and ip -s link shows zero errors/drops/carrier events on router1:eth0 and router2:eth0. The qdisc fq_codel statistics on all involved interfaces show zero drops/overlimits.

From the hosts, sustained ICMP between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) has 0% loss and low, steady RTT. Path MTU is normal (DF 1472 bytes succeeds; 1473 fails as expected with a 1500-byte path). An iperf test achieves ~130–136 Mb/s with some TCP retransmissions but no Layer-2/queue drops, indicating occasional transport-layer dynamics rather than a flapping link.

Localization: The external link router1:eth0 <-> router2:eth0 and the BGP processes on router1/router2 are healthy. There is no evidence of interface flaps, errors, or routing instability.

Root cause: No network-side fault detected; the reported “link flapping” is not supported by current data.

Corrective fix: No reset is needed. Keep the link as-is. Recommend continued monitoring (e.g., 5–10 minute continuous ping/iperf and BGP session logging). If user slowness persists, investigate endpoint/application factors (host load, TCP/windowing, local firewall/AV) since the network path is stable. 
=== END ===